  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Creating local capacity on Solarized Water Powered Systems</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">12 local staff from national societies</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Globally, an estimated 785 million people do not have access to a basic drinking water service. This means they must rely on sources like rivers or lakes &#x2013; prone to contamination &#x2013; or travel more than 30 minutes to collect drinking water. Many solutions provided for water systems are running on costly and polluting fuel. This may also enhance the vulnerability of communities in crisis, as many crisis come with fuel shortages. Solarization of water system is now regarded as a best practice to be streamlined across the movement.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Nairobi, Kenya</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Netherlands Red Cross wants to enable technical local staff to access and master technical knowledge on this subject to promote and execute the switch from fuel to solar powered systems.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aims at training local capacity in national society to shift water systems from diesel fuel to solar</narrative>
  </description>
